WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 16: The Black Bean Lottery of 1843: Texas Soldiers and the Jar of Death

1
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.880
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.080
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:15.440
[Rebecca] In the turbulent decade of the Republic of Texas, border warfare with Mexico was marked by bitter skirmishes, legendary bravery, and terrifying cruelty.

4
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:22.160
[Rebecca] Nowhere was that friction more harrowing than in the spring of 1843, in the remote northern deserts of Mexico.

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:32.080
[Rebecca] Following a rogue military raid across the Rio Grande known as the Mir Expedition, nearly 200 Texian fighters surrendered after running out of ammunition.

6
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:39.280
[Rebecca] Enraged by their defiance, Mexican dictator Antonio Lopez de Santa Ana issued a brutal executive decree.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.720
[Rebecca] Every tenth man was to be shot by a firing squad.

8
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:50.960
[Rebecca] To select the condemned, Mexican officers blindfolded the prisoners and ordered them to reach into an earthen clay jar filled with beans.

9
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.280
[Rebecca] A white bean meant life in prison.

10
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.160
[Rebecca] A black bean meant execution at sunrise.

11
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.440
[George] Here is George with the story.

12
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:13.840
[George] Six years had passed since General Sam Houston defeated Antonio Lopez de Santa Ana at San Jacinto, winning Texian independence.

13
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:23.520
[George] Yet Mexico refused to recognize the treaties, launching sudden cavalry raids across the Rio Grande that captured San Antonio twice in a single year.

14
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:34.640
[George] In response, Texas President Sam Houston authorized General Alexander Somaval to lead 700 volunteers to patrol the border and deter further Mexican incursions.

15
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:47.200
[George] When Somavell determined that an invasion of Mexico was militarily reckless and ordered his men to disband and return home, over 300 hotheaded fronters men mutinied, led by Captain William S.

16
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:59.040
[George] Fisher and Fiery Ranger Ewan Cameron, these men refused to retreat, voting to cross the Rio Grande and assault the Mexican border city of Ciudad Mir to seize supplies and horses.

17
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:12.280
[George] On Christmas Day, 1842, the Texian raiders attacked Mir in the freezing winter rain, engaging over 2,000 Mexican regular infantrymen and cavalry commanded by General Pedro de Emputia.

18
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:20.200
[George] For 24 hours, the Texians fought from house to house with deadly rifle accuracy, inflicting hundreds of casualties.

19
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:34.440
[George] However, cut off from their supply lines, low on water, and completely out of gunpowder, Captain Fisher agreed to an honorable surrender, with General Empudia promising in writing that the Texians would be treated as prisoners of war.

20
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:45.160
[George] The Texian volunteers were armed with Kentucky long rifles and revolving pistols, possessing lethal marksmanship, but carrying only forty rounds of ammunition per man.

21
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.080
[George] The promise of honorable treatment proved to be completely hollow.

22
00:02:49.080 --> 00:03:00.520
[George] Instead of being paroled, the 260 Texian prisoners were bound in heavy iron shackles and marched on foot deep into the interior of Mexico toward the dungeons of Parot Castle.

23
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:13.640
[George] As the column marched under brutal sun through the desert near the hacienda of Salado in Coahuila, the Texians realized that their ultimate fate was likely execution or permanent forced labor in Mexican silver mines.

24
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:21.240
[George] Led by the towering Scottish born ranger Captain Ewan Cameron, the prisoners organized a daring, desperate escape.

25
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:29.880
[George] On the morning of February 11, 1843, as the morning cavalry patrol changed guard, Cameron shouted the secret signal.

26
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.480
[George] Now, boys!

27
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:32.600
[George] Go for them.

28
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:40.200
[George] Unarmed, the Texians charged the Mexican guards, wrestling carbines and sabers from their hands in hand to hand combat.

29
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.560
[George] They overwhelmed the garrison, captured 200 horses, and broke out into the vast Chihuahuan desert.

30
00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:53.280
[George] However, in their desperation to evade pursuing cavalry, the Texians made a fatal navigational error.

31
00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:00.480
[George] Fearing the main road, they abandoned their horses and plunged into the trackless, waterless mountains of the Sierra Matter.

32
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.880
[George] Within days under the scorching desert sun, water ran out.

33
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.960
[George] Men were reduced to eating snakes, cactus roots, and finally drinking their own horses' blood.

34
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:20.800
[George] Delirious, barefoot, and dying of dehydration, 176 exhausted survivors collapsed and were recaptured by Mexican cavalry.

35
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:32.560
[George] Cameron was a legendary Scottish-born frontiersman who had survived countless Indian skirmishes, earning a reputation among both Texians and Mexicans as a warrior of superhuman endurance.

36
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:40.800
[George] When news of the violent breakout reached Mexico City, General Antonio Lopez de Santa Ana flew into a volcanic rage.

37
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:49.840
[George] The dictator viewed the Texians not as legitimate soldiers of a sovereign republic, but as armed filibusters and treasonous pirates who deserved no mercy.

38
00:04:49.840 --> 00:05:00.160
[George] Santa Ana immediately signed a secret executive order commanding that every single recaptured Texian soldier be stood against an adobe wall and shot dead on the spot.

39
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:14.720
[George] However, the governor of Coahuila, Francisco Mexia, and senior field commander General José Maria Mexia were horrified by the order, flatly refusing to carry out an outright mass slaughter of surrendered prisoners of war.

40
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:29.200
[George] Foreign diplomats from Great Britain and the United States also intervened, warning Santa Ana that murdering all 176 men would trigger an international diplomatic crisis and likely provoke an American military invasion.

41
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.920
[George] Furious at having his absolute authority challenged, Santa Ana grudgingly compromised.

42
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.400
[George] He invoked the ancient, brutal Roman military practice of decimation.

43
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:44.200
[George] Instead of executing the entire company, every tenth man would be put to death.

44
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:51.560
[George] Seventeen Texians would die, and their identities would be determined through the pure, horrifying chance of a lottery.

45
00:05:51.560 --> 00:06:02.040
[George] Mexican field officers openly voiced their moral disgust with the execution order, warning that executing captured soldiers violated the customs of civilized nations.

46
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:12.200
[George] On the afternoon of March 25, 1843, the recaptured prisoners were lined up in the central courtyard of the Hacienda Salado under heavy armed guard.

47
00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:18.760
[George] A Mexican colonel named Domingo Huerta placed an earthen clay jar on a wooden bench in the center of the yard.

48
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:29.000
[George] Inside the jar, Huerta dropped precisely 176 dried beans, 159 white beans, and exactly 17 black beans.

49
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:37.880
[George] The beans were identical in size and shape, mixed thoroughly, and covered with a thick silk handkerchief to prevent anyone from seeing inside.

50
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.920
[George] Huerta announced the rules through a translator.

51
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:47.880
[George] Each man would step forward in alphabetical order, reach his bare hand into the jar, and pull out a single bean.

52
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.360
[George] A white bean meant life, chains, and the march to Parot prison.

53
00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.600
[George] A black bean meant immediate death by firing squad at sunset.

54
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.080
[George] The scene was agonizingly tense.

55
00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:07.240
[George] The prisoners were emaciated, filthy, and covered in desert dust, yet they faced the jar with extraordinary frontier defiance.

56
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.800
[George] To ensure fairness, Captain Ewan Cameron was ordered to draw first.

57
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:21.600
[George] Cameron stepped forward with steady hands, reached into the jar, and withdrew a white bean, shouting to his men, Well, boys, I'm safe, however.

58
00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:30.880
[George] Mexican officers had deliberately placed the seventeen black beans near the top of the jar, so the officers who drew early faced catastrophic odds.

59
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:42.720
[George] Father Ramon, a local Catholic priest from the nearby mission, walked among the prisoners, offering the last rites of the church and praying for the souls of whoever would draw the fatal black beans.

60
00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.920
[George] One by one, the Texian prisoners stepped forward to draw their fate from the jar.

61
00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:50.240
[George] Men displayed astonishing courage.

62
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:59.760
[George] When Captain William Eastland drew a black bean, he held it up to the afternoon sun, smiled wryly, and remarked that he had always been unlucky in lotteries.

63
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:07.520
[George] A seventeen-year-old boy named Robert Beard was trembling in terror, but his older friend stepped forward to comfort him.

64
00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:20.960
[George] Major Robert Dunham, a pious young man from Tennessee, asked the Mexican officers for a pencil and paper, writing a farewell letter to his mother in which he stated that he faced death without fear and forgave his captors.

65
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.080
[George] By four o'clock in the afternoon, all seventeen black beans had been drawn.

66
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:36.640
[George] The seventeen condemned Texians were immediately separated from their comrades, placed in heavy leg irons, and moved into a small adobe room on the southern side of the courtyard.

67
00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:46.320
[George] The survivors who had drawn white beans wept openly, embracing their condemned friends through the barred windows, sharing their last tobacco scraps and prayers.

68
00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:53.760
[George] The seventeen men requested that they be shot in the front rather than the back, so they could face their executioners like soldiers.

69
00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.880
[George] At dusk, Mexican infantry formed firing squads behind the adobe walls.

70
00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:05.720
[George] Volleys of musketry echoed through the desert canyon, extinguishing seventeen Texian lives in the twilight.

71
00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:15.400
[George] As each shot echoed through the courtyard, the surviving Texians knelt in prayer, holding each other's hands and reciting the 23rd Psalm in the fading daylight.

72
00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:24.840
[George] The surviving Texians were forced to resume their grueling march across Mexico, ultimately reaching the colossal fortress of Castle Perot in Veracruz.

73
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.760
[George] Even there, Santa Ana was not finished with his vengeance.

74
00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:41.240
[George] Furious that the defiant Scottish captain Ewan Cameron had survived the lottery, Santa Ana issued a special order for Cameron to be executed by a separate firing squad while imprisoned at Huhuatoka.

75
00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:48.680
[George] Cameron stood before the rifles, refused a blindfold, bared his chest, and gave the command to fire himself.

76
00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.360
[George] Over the next two years, international diplomatic pressure mounted from Washington and London.

77
00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:03.960
[George] In September of 1844, Santa Ana finally released the surviving Texian prisoners, who returned home to Texas as celebrated martyrs.

78
00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:13.960
[George] The Black Bean Lottery became an indelible part of the Texas founding legend, immortalized in paintings, songs, and historical memorials across the state.

79
00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:31.880
[George] In 1848, after the Mexican American War, the bones of the seventeen men executed at Salado were exhumed by Texas Rangers, returned to Texas with full military honors, and buried in a massive stone tomb on Monument Hill in La Grange, overlooking the Colorado River.

80
00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:43.000
[George] The story of the earthen jar and the black beans remains an enduring testament to the grit, brotherhood, and unflinching frontier courage of those who dared carve a nation out of the wilderness.

81
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:54.960
[George] Today, Monument Hill's state Historic Site preserves the stone tomb containing the remains of the 17 heroes of the Black Bean Lottery, overlooking the rolling hills of Central Texas.

82
00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:02.080
[Rebecca] The Black Bean Lottery of 1843 stands as one of the most chilling, cinematic moments of frontier history.

83
00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:12.960
[Rebecca] It reminds us that the line between life and death on the frontier often came down to the luck of the draw, and that true courage is not the absence of fear, but facing the earthen jar with an unyielding heart.

84
00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.680
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

85
00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

86
00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.160
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

87
00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.560
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

